{"name":"Light Pearl Ash","hex":"#dcd6d1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"light","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye (about 400–750 nanometers): visible light.","Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye or in nearby ranges (infrared or ultraviolet radiation)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To start (a fire).","To set fire to; to set burning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/light"},{"term":"pearl","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shelly concretion, usually rounded, and having a brilliant luster, with varying tints, found in the mantle, or between the mantle and shell, of certain bivalve mollusks, especially in the pearl oysters and river mussels, and sometimes in certain univalves. It is usually due to a secretion of shelly substance around some irritating foreign particle. Its substance is the same as nacre, or mother-of-pearl. Round lustrous pearls are used in jewellery.\nNacre; mother-of-pearl.\n A white color inspired by the shade of pearls.","Nacre; mother-of-pearl."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To set or adorn with pearls, or with mother-of-pearl.","To cause to resemble pearls in shape; to make into small round grains."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pearl"},{"term":"ash","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Abishira."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The solid remains of a fire.","The nonaqueous remains of a material subjected to any complete oxidation process."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ash"}],"entries":[{"title":"Potash","blocks":[{"text":"Potash is a name for mined and manufactured salts that contain potassium in water-soluble form."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/PotashUSGOV.jpg/330px-PotashUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Potash","wikidata":"Q10564271"},{"title":"Pearl","blocks":[{"text":"A pearl is a hard, shiny object produced within the soft tissue of a living shelled mollusk. Like the shell of a mollusk, a pearl is composed of calcium carbonate in a crystalline form, which is deposited in concentric layers. More commercially valuable pearls are perfectly round and smooth, but many other shapes, known as baroque pearls, can occur. The highest quality of natural pearls have been highly valued as gemstones and objects of beauty for many centuries. In literature, pearls have become a metaphor for something rare, fine, admirable, and valuable."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Various_pearls.jpg/330px-Various_pearls.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pearl","wikidata":"Q43436"},{"title":"Ash","blocks":[{"text":"Ash is the solid remnants of fires. Specifically, ash refers to all non-aqueous, non-gaseous residues that remain after something burns. In analytical chemistry, to analyse the mineral and metal content of chemical samples, ash is the non-gaseous, non-liquid residue after complete combustion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Wood_ash.jpg/330px-Wood_ash.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ash","wikidata":"Q152079"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined as having wavelengths in the range of 400–700 nanometers (nm), corresponding to frequencies of 750–420 terahertz. The visible band sits adjacent to the infrared and the ultraviolet, called collectively optical radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light","wikidata":"Q9128"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/First_United_States_of_America_patent_%28July_31%2C_1790%29.jpg/500px-First_United_States_of_America_patent_%28July_31%2C_1790%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/First_United_States_of_America_patent_%28July_31%2C_1790%29.jpg/1280px-First_United_States_of_America_patent_%28July_31%2C_1790%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/First_United_States_of_America_patent_%28July_31%2C_1790%29.jpg/500px-First_United_States_of_America_patent_%28July_31%2C_1790%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The very first U.S. patent ever to be  issued was for an improvement \"in the making of Pot ash and Pearl ash by a new Apparatus and Process\"; it was signed by then President George Washington.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFirst_United_States_of_America_patent_(July_31%2C_1790).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/CoveredHopperForPotashBoltonON.jpg/500px-CoveredHopperForPotashBoltonON.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/CoveredHopperForPotashBoltonON.jpg/1280px-CoveredHopperForPotashBoltonON.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/CoveredHopperForPotashBoltonON.jpg/500px-CoveredHopperForPotashBoltonON.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A covered hopper ca